# G22 — support-corner slack, radius freedom, and the literal parity bridge

This pass closes the algebraic sublane of the odd finite-radius chain, answers
the radius-freedom audit, builds the literal test-function bridge, and settles
the single-frequency four-sample decision.  Everything below is machine-checked
in Lean 4 / Mathlib under `lake build RiemannAnalytic`, with no `sorry`, no
`axiom`, no postulate, no Boolean receipt, no numerical evidence, and
`#print axioms` reporting only `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`.

## 1. The support-corner slack identity (`RiemannAnalytic/OddTaylorSupportSlack.lean`)

With the normalised determinant-level error polynomial

    P(q_a, q_p, c_a, c_p) = 204 (c_a + c_p) + 40 (q_a c_p + q_p c_a) + 64 c_a c_p

and the compact-support majorant `S(R) = 510 R⁴ + 100 R⁶ + 4 R⁸`:

* `supportMajorant_eq_corner_value` — `S(R) = 25 · P(R², R², R⁴/20, R⁴/20)`.
  The majorant is *literally* the corner value, not a loose estimate.
* `oddTaylorSupportPolynomialSlackIdentity` — the exact ring identity

      S − 25P = 25 [ 40C((U − q_a) + (U − q_p))
                     + (C − c_a)(204 + 40 q_p + 64C)
                     + (C − c_p)(204 + 40 q_a + 64 c_a) ],
      U = R²,  C = R⁴/20,

  recording the four debts to saturation explicitly.
* `oddTaylorSupportPolynomialMajorization` — the ordered consequence
  `25 P ≤ S` under exactly `q ≤ R²`, `20 c ≤ R⁴` and nonnegativity.  No
  optimisation theorem, no derivative, no multivariate monotonicity.
* `oddTaylorRadiusGate` — `S r² < 900 Δ ⟹ P r² < 36 Δ`.

## 2. Radius freedom — audit answered

The audit question was whether the observation radius `r` is free to be chosen
after `a, p, R`, or constrained elsewhere.

**It is free.**  Every downstream statement in the zero-side chain is of the
form `∃ r₀ > 0, ∀ r, 0 < r → r < r₀ → …`:
`exists_radius_oddFiniteRadiusDeterminant_neg`,
`exists_radius_evenFiniteRadiusDeterminant_neg`,
`exists_radius_both_parity_minors_neg`, `parity_responses_linearly_independent`,
`pole_quotient_transversality`, `zero_response_not_in_pole_span`,
`offline_zero_parity_transversality`.  Consequently:

* `exists_threshold_supportMajorant_lt` — `Δ > 0`, `S ≥ 0` gives a threshold
  below which `S r² < 900 Δ`.
* `NormalizedMomentCoefficients.strictTP2_exists_radius_strict_odd_minor` — from
  *strict* TP₂ positivity alone (`q_p − q_a > 0`) one obtains a radius threshold
  below which the support gate, the normalised gate, and strict negativity of
  the actual finite-radius odd minor all hold simultaneously.

So **no quantitative lower bound on the TP₂ margin is required anywhere**.

